      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "tools/narrative-queue/narrative-queue.json#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "tool.selection.when",
      "prov:value": "To find out what is waiting on a person, or to discover that this queue exists at all — which was the actual gap. Before this node, `grep narrative tools/*.ts` returned nothing, so an agent asking the graph how a person confirms a narrative got no answer, and that is exactly the population the command is for."
    },
    {
      "@id": "tools/narrative-queue/narrative-queue.json#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "tool.selection.limits",
      "prov:value": "That refusal is load-bearing rather than defensive — driving the CLI in an agent container once wrote `\"rejected_by\": {\"kind\": \"human\", \"id\": \"Claude\"}`, because `git config user.name` is the agent's and the schema could not tell. So this node deliberately does not offer the confirming arms: an agent may read the queue and must not answer it. A rejection with no reason is refused too, not defaulted, because one lets the next agent redraft the identical thing."
    }
  ]
}
